WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.044
A packaging plant.

2
00:00:02.827 --> 00:00:06.716
Every stop over 2 minutes goes on a new terminal by the line.

3
00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:10.523
Then go-live, the day it officially starts.

4
00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.864
After it, the old way tempts, and problems show up.

5
00:00:15.995 --> 00:00:18.793
After six weeks: only 40 percent logged.

6
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:24.454
Go-live is not adoption: actually using it, also when nobody watches.

7
00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.864
Getting people there is change management.

8
00:00:28.906 --> 00:00:34.026
What drives it: easier for whoever does it, the boss asks, they helped shape it,

9
00:00:34.247 --> 00:00:35.420
the old way is gone.

10
00:00:36.424 --> 00:00:39.850
If it takes the operator longer, no campaign saves it.

11
00:00:41.193 --> 00:00:42.052
Two models.

12
00:00:42.759 --> 00:00:48.518
Kotter, in Leading Change, 1996: eight steps for the organisation,

13
00:00:49.491 --> 00:00:53.740
from urgency and a group that drives it, to quick wins and anchoring.

14
00:00:54.732 --> 00:00:58.877
ADKAR, by Jeff Hiatt of Prosci, is for one person: Awareness,

15
00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.034
Desire, Knowledge, Ability, Reinforcement, staying rewarded.

16
00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:10.690
Critique: a 2011 review by Mark Hughes found no solid evidence that 70 percent fail.

17
00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:13.134
Kotter is called too top-down.

18
00:01:14.472 --> 00:01:16.492
Use ADKAR as a diagnosis.

19
00:01:17.238 --> 00:01:18.956
Not aware: the boss tells them.

20
00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:21.976
No desire: find what they lose.

21
00:01:22.718 --> 00:01:24.216
No knowledge: explain.

22
00:01:24.921 --> 00:01:26.546
No ability: practise.

23
00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:29.970
No reinforcement: someone asks weekly.

24
00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:34.154
All eight operators asked know about it, and how.

25
00:01:35.211 --> 00:01:37.568
But three say nothing is ever done with it.

26
00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:41.560
For two, the terminal is 30 metres from the stops.

27
00:01:42.582 --> 00:01:45.322
The shift lead opens each shift start with

28
00:01:45.391 --> 00:01:48.294
yesterday's top three stops and what happens to them;

29
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.616
maintenance moves the terminal to the middle.

30
00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.958
Four weeks later: 85 percent.

31
00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:59.862
Give the reason in their terms: not OEE, but no call-in on Saturday.

32
00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:03.028
Ask five people what makes it hard.

33
00:02:03.792 --> 00:02:05.789
And: what does the operator lose?

34
00:02:07.146 --> 00:02:10.409
Resistance: people slow a change down, or block it.

35
00:02:11.473 --> 00:02:15.049
Open resistance: arguments, anger, this won't work.

36
00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:19.356
Passive resistance: yes in the meeting, nothing after.

37
00:02:20.062 --> 00:02:20.712
Too busy.

38
00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:22.507
Missed sessions.

39
00:02:23.521 --> 00:02:29.395
Hidden resistance: tried that in 2019, the old spreadsheet kept alive,

40
00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:31.067
data that never arrives.

41
00:02:32.105 --> 00:02:35.530
Open resistance is a gift: it shows what to work on.

42
00:02:36.272 --> 00:02:38.199
The other two you must find first.

43
00:02:39.594 --> 00:02:44.203
Resistance tells you something real: a loss, like status or overtime,

44
00:02:44.378 --> 00:02:48.755
extra work, a failed project they paid for, or a flaw in your design.

45
00:02:49.793 --> 00:02:53.694
Ask before you judge: what would have to be true for this to work for you?

46
00:02:54.708 --> 00:02:57.297
When a loss is real, say so honestly.

47
00:02:58.003 --> 00:03:01.997
And never label anyone a resister; it sticks and closes the door.

48
00:03:03.007 --> 00:03:07.407
The change curve, from shock to acceptance, is borrowed from Kübler-Ross,

49
00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:10.913
1969, on dying patients.

50
00:03:11.613 --> 00:03:14.493
A dip is normal; fixed stages, unproven.

51
00:03:15.515 --> 00:03:18.556
With Heifetz: people resist loss, not change.

52
00:03:19.288 --> 00:03:21.401
Resistance is not a bad attitude.

53
00:03:22.736 --> 00:03:23.769
A metal works.

54
00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:29.012
Henk, the senior setter, calls the new board for office people; arms crossed,

55
00:03:29.151 --> 00:03:30.231
his colleagues follow.

56
00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:35.245
You have coffee with him, one-on-one, and ask about earlier changes.

57
00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:39.082
He used to set the order; now the planner does,

58
00:03:39.244 --> 00:03:42.402
unaware the big press needs 40 minutes to warm up.

59
00:03:43.506 --> 00:03:48.196
Invite the sharpest critic into the design: Henk sets the rules with the planner,

60
00:03:48.289 --> 00:03:49.589
warm-up rule included.

61
00:03:50.587 --> 00:03:54.360
Two weeks later he explains the board to the night shift himself.

62
00:03:55.386 --> 00:04:02.271
In 1948, Coch and French saw it at a pyjama factory: co-designers recovered faster,

63
00:04:02.457 --> 00:04:03.432
and quit less.

64
00:04:04.884 --> 00:04:10.620
Map the stakeholders, affected or influential: directly, indirectly, and managers.

65
00:04:11.642 --> 00:04:15.950
Five questions per group: what changes, when, why, what is expected,

66
00:04:16.065 --> 00:04:16.878
where can I ask?

67
00:04:17.621 --> 00:04:20.442
That one-page table: your communication plan.

68
00:04:21.448 --> 00:04:25.779
Managers hear it before their teams, so they have answers: a cascade.

69
00:04:26.522 --> 00:04:29.308
Never first in the corridor or from another site.

70
00:04:30.351 --> 00:04:31.117
A rumour out?

71
00:04:31.878 --> 00:04:35.105
The same day: what we know, what not yet, and when.

72
00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:38.290
The key channel: the middle manager.

73
00:04:39.016 --> 00:04:43.370
The top gives the why; the shift lead hears: what does this mean for my Monday?

74
00:04:44.378 --> 00:04:49.603
Equip them first: their questions answered, three talking points in their own words,

75
00:04:49.824 --> 00:04:51.670
likely questions with answers.

76
00:04:52.706 --> 00:04:55.086
Repeat: once is not communication.

77
00:04:55.843 --> 00:05:00.115
A town hall gives the why; then the board, the shift start, one-on-one.

78
00:05:01.122 --> 00:05:07.310
Kotter estimated in 1995 that leaders under-communicate their vision tenfold.

79
00:05:08.359 --> 00:05:11.018
Prosci's surveys: the reason from the top,

80
00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:13.851
what it means for your job from your own boss.

81
00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.723
A grid operator: no job enters the week plan without a readiness check.

82
00:05:20.441 --> 00:05:23.146
The head of planning wants one Friday e-mail.

83
00:05:24.152 --> 00:05:28.762
Better: in week 1 the five team leaders hear it first, with an FAQ.

84
00:05:29.523 --> 00:05:31.601
In week 2 they tell their planners.

85
00:05:32.651 --> 00:05:35.043
Weeks 3 and 4: the check on the board.

86
00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:39.161
Six planners asked what they understood; two think it optional.

87
00:05:40.193 --> 00:05:42.167
A cascade often stops halfway.

88
00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:46.044
A week on, ask: who have you told, and what did they ask?

89
00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:47.376
Don't know?

90
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:48.977
Say when you will.
